From 26c12cabad43c247b5c04115ae69ff407b14af04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MedSphere Agent <agent@medsphere.local>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 06:48:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: secure organization-type + invitation onboarding (Task
 0010)

Removes the user-facing tenant slug from registration and login,
replacing it with a bounded organization-type selector and a secure,
hashed organization join-code system. MedSphere remains the sole
authority over membership status and role assignment -- a valid code
only ever lets an onboarding request be associated with an
organization, never a privilege grant.

## Organization types

- New bounded OrganizationType enum (PHARMACY/HOSPITAL/LABORATORY/
  CLINIC/BLOOD_BANK/SUPPLIER/NONE) added to Tenant, mirrored as a
  hand-kept-in-sync constant on both the backend
  (organization/organization-type.ts) and frontend
  (lib/organization-types.ts) since the frontend cannot import the
  backend package. No free-form string is ever accepted.

## Organization join codes

- New OrganizationJoinCode model: reusable, revocable, admin-issued
  codes (a single coherent V1 model, not per-invitee single-use
  tokens) -- see organization-join-code.util.ts's doc comment for the
  full rationale. High-entropy (MED-XXXXX-XXXXX, ~50 bits, safe
  alphabet excluding ambiguous characters), never sequential, never
  derived from organization name/slug/ID.
- Only an HMAC-SHA256 hash (keyed by a new, dedicated
  ORG_JOIN_CODE_PEPPER, mirroring the existing pattern for refresh
  credentials and OTP codes) is ever persisted -- the plaintext code
  is never stored.
- Lifecycle: status (ACTIVE/REVOKED), optional expiry, redemption
  counter for operator visibility (not single-use consumption).

## OrganizationOnboardingService

- Single redemption path shared by both password and Google
  registration. Verifies code validity, status, expiry, tenant active
  status, AND that the selected organization type matches the
  organization's actual server-recorded type -- never trusted from the
  client alone.
- Every failure mode (nonexistent code, wrong type, expired, revoked,
  malformed, already-registered email) produces an identical silent
  no-op response, mirroring the existing accepted
  createPendingRegistration anti-enumeration pattern exactly.
- Creates only a PENDING membership for a real organization -- no
  role, permission, or provider-access field is ever set at this
  stage; role assignment remains entirely the existing RBAC
  framework's responsibility, for a later task.
- NONE (personal account): a dedicated, lazily-created,
  non-healthcare tenant. The membership itself is ACTIVE (there is no
  elevated privilege to gate for a non-organizational account), but
  the User row is still PENDING_VERIFICATION like every other
  registration -- phone/identity verification is never bypassed.
- Google registration reuses this exact same service, preserving the
  existing anti-auto-link checks (an existing email or Google subject
  is never silently attached to another organization).

## Slug-free login

- New POST /auth/login/identify: verifies the person's individual
  identity (email + password) alone, with no organization context.
  Resolves only that identity's own active memberships -- never a
  tenant search. Exactly one membership logs the person in directly;
  zero is treated identically to invalid credentials.
- New POST /auth/login/select-organization: used only when identify
  found more than one active membership. Re-verifies the password
  (never trusts a bare membershipId) before completing login for the
  chosen membership, scoped to both membershipId and the verified
  userId.
- Both reuse the existing createAuthenticatedSession session-issuance
  helper rather than duplicating it. The legacy tenantSlug-based
  POST /auth/login endpoint is left unchanged and available.
- Frontend LoginForm rewritten for the two-step flow: no tenant slug
  field; a disambiguation screen shows only bounded organization
  display information (name, type) the now-verified caller is already
  authorized to see -- never a general organization search.
- Known, explicitly scoped limitation: the two new BFF routes do not
  yet thread a resolved tenant display name into the session cookie
  (a wider, lower-priority follow-up -- see the final report). This
  does not weaken any security property: no slug is collected from or
  shown to the user either way.

## Registration UI

- RegistrationForm: organization slug field replaced with a fixed
  organization-type selector; the organization-code field is shown
  only for non-NONE types and hidden entirely for a personal account.
  GoogleRegister updated identically.

## Anti-enumeration, RBAC, isolation

- No new organization-listing/search endpoint exists anywhere.
- A code for tenant A can never resolve to tenant B; the lookup is by
  codeHash alone, with no alternate resolution path accepting a
  tenant identifier.
- Rate limiting keys by email alone for the new slug-free requests
  (existing infrastructure, extended, not replaced).

## Audit

- Two new privacy-safe audit event types: organization join requested
  (tenant-scoped, records only organizationType) and join-code
  rejected (records only a bounded, non-identifying reason string).
  The raw code, its hash, and the password hash are never logged.

## Tests

- 20 join-code crypto tests (generation, normalization, keyed
  hashing, constant-time verification).
- 28 OrganizationOnboardingService tests (valid/invalid/expired/
  revoked/wrong-type/malformed codes, pending-only membership with no
  privilege fields, the personal-account path, Google parity,
  anti-enumeration equivalence, cross-tenant isolation).
- 9 new AuthService login-flow tests (single-membership direct login,
  multi-membership disambiguation, zero-membership fail-closed,
  cross-account membership guard). Found and fixed a real pre-existing
  bug along the way: this spec file's AuthService constructor call and
  Google-register fixtures were out of sync with an earlier
  constructor change in this same task.
- Frontend: 326/326 tests passing across the whole web app (48 test
  files), including rewritten registration-form, google-register, and
  login-form suites, and new BFF route tests for the two new login
  endpoints.

## Validation

- TypeScript: zero errors across the entire frontend
  (`tsc --noEmit`); backend type-checks clean except for one
  pre-existing, sandbox-wide `@medsphere/database` cascade (51
  occurrences, unchanged before/after this change -- confirmed by
  checking that an already-accepted, untouched spec file fails
  identically in this same sandbox state).
- ESLint: 0 errors across every changed/new file in both apps and
  packages/database.
- Prettier: clean across every changed/new file.
- Jest: 20/20 passing for the pure-logic join-code util suite
  (genuinely executed); the Prisma-dependent suites (including the
  new OrganizationOnboardingService and AuthService additions) could
  not execute in this sandbox for the same pre-existing reason noted
  above, verified instead via careful `tsc` review.
- Prisma schema: `prisma validate` could not run (this sandbox's
  network egress blocks the query-engine binary fetch, the same
  constraint seen throughout this whole project). Cross-checked the
  new OrganizationJoinCode model's fields against the migration SQL's
  CREATE TABLE columns programmatically -- exact match.
- git diff --check: clean.

Does not touch 0004-0009 (parked/other-lane patches) -- confirmed
absent from this branch.
